161	        // The URL comes from a client-authored document, so it gets exactly the transport
162	        // policy client_id resolution uses: https, no credentials — with loopback as the one
163	        // place plain http is acceptable. Holding `jwks_uri` to a stricter rule than the
164	        // `client_id` it was fetched from would make a loopback development client
165	        // (explicitly supported by the spec, and by `oauth_client_resolution`) unable to
166	        // publish its keys by URL at all.
167	        //
168	        // The scheme check is not the SSRF control and never was: that is the hardened
169	        // client's connect-time resolver, which refuses loopback and private addresses in

