2	//
3	// Every other file in this suite asserts what *we* concluded the protocol requires. That is
4	// the same reasoning that produced five production bugs: our tests agreed with our server
5	// because the same understanding wrote both. `@atproto/oauth-client-node` encodes Bluesky's
6	// reading instead, and it fails on things we would not think to assert — so its value here is
7	// precisely that we did not write it.
8	//
9	// Consequently this file makes almost no assertions of its own. It runs the SDK's own flow and
10	// lets the SDK object. A failure here usually means the server is wrong, not the test.
11	//
12	// It also exercises checks nothing else in the suite reaches: the SDK resolves the `sub` DID
13	// it is handed, finds that DID's PDS, and verifies the tokens really came from there — the
14	// check that stops a hostile authorization server minting tokens for someone else's account.
15	// That is why this fixture needs a directory that can answer a DID lookup (see mock-plc.ts).
